Resolve the associated counter when a structured buffer is assigned from a
descriptor heap.

Applies to: v1.10.2605.37 (c4d8f4f9). Also applies to main as of 2026-08-28.

Assigning a counter-bearing resource out of ResourceDescriptorHeap fails with
"cannot handle associated counter variable assignment", while initialising one
from the same expression works:

    RWStructuredBuffer<T> a = ResourceDescriptorHeap[i];   // works
    s.buffer                = ResourceDescriptorHeap[i];   // fails

It stops five of this engine's shaders. AppendBuffer.esh builds its
AppendBuffer<T> exactly the second way, and two more failures are not in
hand-written shader source at all: the Reflector's code generator emits the
same pattern when it builds a resource table struct.

tryToAssignCounterVar requires the source and the destination to agree on
whether they have a counter. A struct field gets one eagerly, from
createFieldCounterVars; the heap access resolves to none, so they disagree.
The source never resolves for three separate reasons, and all three have to be
fixed for the assignment to work.

1. getFinalACSBufferCounter tests AssocCounter#1 first. heap[i] is a
   CXXOperatorCallExpr, so getReferencedDef resolves it to the subscript
   operator, and the function returns that operator's empty counter pair
   before it ever reaches the descriptor heap branch below. The heap branch is
   moved above AssocCounter#1.

2. That branch tested isResourceDescriptorHeap against the expression's own
   type. The expression's type is the indexed resource; isResourceDescriptorHeap
   wants the type of the heap object being indexed. isDescriptorHeap is the
   predicate that matches the expression, and getDescriptorHeapOperands already
   asserts on it.

3. Even with both corrected, the counter is resolved before the right-hand
   side is emitted. A descriptor heap access creates the heap's SPIR-V variable
   as a side effect of being emitted, and that is what registers the heap as a
   structured buffer that can own a counter. doBinaryOperator therefore
   evaluates the rhs before calling tryToAssignCounterVar. Its own comment
   already says "we need to evaluate rhs before lhs"; the counter call sat
   above the evaluation.

A fourth point is needed to avoid over-reporting. One heap declaration is
shared by every resource type read out of it, and registering the RW variant
puts that declaration into declRWSBuffers. Without a type test, a later read of
a StructuredBuffer or an RWBuffer out of the same heap reports the
RWStructuredBuffer's counter, and the assignment is then rejected against a
destination that rightly has no counter. The resource type a heap access
produces sits on the implicit cast above the subscript, which is where
doCXXOperatorCallExpr already reads it from, so getDescriptorHeapResourceType
reads it from the same place.

Effect on this engine: all 46 shader stages compile, up from 41. The 41 that
compiled before produce byte-identical SPIR-V. No shader emits a counter heap,
because none of them use an append or consume counter.

Effect on DXC's own tests: 1343 of 1357 CodeGenSPIRV tests pass, against 1345
before. The two are spirv.legal.sbuffer.struct.hlsl and
spirv.legal.sbuffer.usage.hlsl, and both fail only on CHECK-NEXT lines that
assume the counter store is emitted before the right-hand side rather than
after. The opcode histograms of both are identical to the unpatched compiler's,
so the change is a reordering. Upstream would want those two tests updated;
this patch deliberately does not touch them, so that the fork's diff against
the pinned tag stays confined to the fix.

